---

<Doc
  title="Methodology"
  description="Where each value on providers.dev comes from, how the models.dev baseline is merged with corrections and evaluations, and what unknown means."
  lead="The catalog is the latest verified import of models.dev, plus added providers, documented corrections and independent evaluations. This page explains how they are combined and what each label means."
  toc={[
    { id: "kinds", label: "Kinds of value" },
    { id: "unknown", label: "Unknown values" },
    { id: "limits", label: "Limits" },
    { id: "prices", label: "Prices" },
    { id: "access", label: "Access methods" },
    { id: "merge", label: "Merge rules" },
    { id: "sync", label: "Daily import" },
    { id: "caching", label: "Documented caching" },
    { id: "evaluations", label: "Cache evaluations" },
    { id: "lifetimes", label: "Cache lifetimes" },
    { id: "stale", label: "Dates and staleness" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="kinds">Kinds of value</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><span class="origin upstream">models.dev</span> <strong>Imported.</strong> Copied from models.dev and not independently checked. Imported values never carry a verification date.</li>
      <li><span class="badge documented">documented</span> <strong>Documented.</strong> Set because the provider's own documentation says so. The entry names the page and the date it was read.</li>
      <li><span class="badge measured">measured</span> <strong>Measured.</strong> Observed by sending requests. The entry names the access method, the client, the date and the raw result.</li>
      <li><strong>API price.</strong> The list price of the API entry of the same model, shown on a subscription entry and marked "API price". It is the only value an entry takes from another one (see <a href="#prices">Prices</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A documented value and a measured one are both shown, each with its source and date. A provider documenting a 30-minute cache and an evaluation that found the cache gone after
      15 minutes are two different facts, and both stay published. A measured value takes the place of a documented one in one figure only, the safe cache lifetime, and only where a
      measurement clearly contradicts the documentation (see <a href="#lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</a>); the documented lifetime stays listed beside it. The other exception is an added
      entry, where no documented limit exists: there a measured limit is published as the limit and labelled as measured.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unknown">Unknown values</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A missing limit is <em>unknown</em>. It is not zero and not unlimited, and it never matches a minimum filter.</li>
      <li>A missing price is unknown, not free. A missing release date is unknown too, and such an entry never matches the "Released" filter.</li>
      <li>For caching, models.dev distinguishes <code>cache = false</code> (the entry does not cache) from no <code>cache</code> field (nothing is stated). Both are kept: "None" and "Unknown".</li>
      <li>A field can be published as explicitly unknown where the upstream value does not hold for an access method. The field is then omitted and the reason is recorded.</li>
      <li>
        Every entry page has the same sections, the same tables and the same rows, so that two entries can be read side by side. A row with no value is not left out. It says one of four
        things: <em>Unknown</em> (no source gives the value), <em>Unsupported</em> (a source says the entry cannot do it), <em>Not evaluated</em> (providers.dev has not measured it) or{" "}
        <em>Not applicable</em> (the field has no meaning for this entry). A subscription entry with no API price to refer to says <em>In plan</em>: there is no price per token.
        A list with nothing in it, such as the corrections of an entry, says <em>None</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The API follows the same rule. Every object providers.dev publishes has all of its fields on every entry: an unknown value is <code>null</code>, never a missing field
        (<a href="/docs/api/#unknown">unknown values in the API</a>).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Context window</strong> (<code>limit.context</code>): what this provider and access method accepts for the model. It can be lower than the model's window at its lab; model pages show them side by side.</li>
      <li><strong>Largest input</strong> (<code>limit.input</code>): stated where the input limit is lower than the window.</li>
      <li><strong>Maximum output</strong> (<code>limit.output</code>): the most one response can hold. It is not the default a provider applies when none is requested.</li>
      <li><strong>Usable context</strong>: the window minus the system prompt and tool definitions of the client in use. That overhead varies, so no usable figure is published.</li>
      <li><strong>Compaction threshold</strong>: a setting of an agent or client. It is not a provider limit and is not published as one.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measured input</strong>: where no window is documented and an evaluation found the input limit, the catalog shows it as <span class="limit-value">≥ 915K</span> input: the
        largest input the evaluation saw accepted, rounded down. The entry page gives both ends, the largest accepted and the smallest refused. It is an input limit, not a window: the output
        comes on top. A window listed by a client (a default the client works with) is not taken as the limit.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="prices">Prices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Prices are list prices in US dollars, as models.dev records them (<code>cost</code>). They are imported, not checked.</li>
      <li>
        Text is priced per million tokens. Audio, images, video and web search are charges of their own, each in the unit the source gives: per million tokens or characters, per second
        or minute, per image or megapixel, and per 1,000 web searches. The entry page lists them under the text charges, and the catalog shows one where an entry has no text price.
      </li>
      <li>
        The catalog shows the base price of text input and output, and marks an entry that has conditional rates. Those are on the entry page, one block per condition with the price of each
        charge: a context size ("Context above 272,000 tokens"), a service tier, a region, a date from which a price changes, or the time of day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Peak and off-peak hours.</strong> Some providers charge by the hour or the day of the week. The base price is the price outside the window. A rule that raises it in its
        window is marked <em>Peak hours</em>, one that lowers it <em>Off-peak hours</em>, with the window in UTC and the resulting prices.
      </li>
      <li>
        A conditional rate holds for the whole request that matches its condition: a request above a context threshold is priced at the higher rate as a whole, not only for the part
        above it. That is how models.dev defines its rules, and the order in which they apply: base prices, then its overrides in order, then every matching multiplier. Where a multiplier
        applies, the page shows the price it makes of each base price and the factor. It does not combine several conditions into one bill.
      </li>
      <li>A cache write price that differs by lifetime is shown with each lifetime.</li>
      <li>A price of 0 is shown as <code>$0</code>. It can mean free of charge, or paid for another way (a plan or a seat): the page does not decide.</li>
      <li>
        A subscription is not paid per token. So that a client can still work out what its usage is worth, every subscription entry shows the API list price of the same model, marked{" "}
        <em>API price</em>, and its page names the API entry the price is from. That is the entry a providers.dev entry was added from, or else the entry of the lab's own provider for the
        same canonical model. It is never what the subscription costs. Where the catalog has no API entry of that model, the entry says <em>In plan</em>; a price the source lists for it all
        the same is kept and marked <em>Listed price</em>. Plans and their prices are not listed.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="access">Access methods</h2>
    <p>
      The same model reached with an API key and with a subscription can have different limits and cache behaviour. Each access method is a separate entry under its own provider, linked to the
      same model by <code>canonical_id</code>. An added entry takes only identity from the upstream entry it names
      ({IDENTITY_FIELDS.map((field, i) => <><code>{field}</code>{i < IDENTITY_FIELDS.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>)}). Limits, caching and capabilities are never copied across access
      methods: on an added entry a value appears only when there is evidence for that access method. The one exception is the price: a subscription is not paid per token, so a
      subscription entry shows the API list price of the same model (<code>providers_dev.pricing</code> has <code>billing: reference</code> and names the API entry in{" "}
      <code>reference_ref</code>), so that a client can work out what the usage is worth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Access is a classification: API key, subscription, or unclassified. Plans, plan prices, allowances and quotas are not tracked. API prices are kept as imported. Where a service does not
      disclose which underlying model it serves, the entry has no <code>canonical_id</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="merge">Merge rules</h2>
    <p>The published dataset is computed from three separate inputs on every build. Nothing is edited in place.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The upstream snapshot</strong>: the last import of models.dev that passed validation.</li>
      <li><strong>Additions</strong>: providers and entries that models.dev does not have.</li>
      <li><strong>Corrections</strong>: single fields set on upstream entries. They take precedence.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>A correction is a patch applied field by field:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll max-w-[62rem]">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">In the correction</th><th scope="col">Published</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Field not mentioned</th><td>The upstream value, which keeps following upstream</td><td>Setting <code>limit.context</code> leaves <code>limit.output</code> tracking models.dev</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A value, including <code>false</code> and <code>0</code></th><td>The corrected value</td><td><code>temperature: false</code> is published as false, not dropped</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">An object</th><td>Merged key by key</td><td><code>{`cache: { explicit: { ttl: ["30m"] } }`}</code> keeps upstream's <code>max_breakpoints</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">An array</th><td>Replaces the upstream array</td><td><code>ttl: ["30m"]</code> over <code>["5m", "1h"]</code> gives <code>["30m"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>null</code></th><td>Explicitly unknown: the field is omitted and marked unknown</td><td><code>limit.context: null</code> where the upstream figure does not apply to this access method</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>"$delete"</code></th><td>Removed: the field does not apply</td><td><code>limit.input: "$delete"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>exclude: true</code></th><td>The entry or provider is left out and listed as excluded</td><td>A provider that has shut down</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li>If models.dev changes a corrected field, the correction stays. The entry's provenance shows the new upstream value next to it.</li>
      <li>If a correction is removed, the current upstream value is published again at the next build.</li>
      <li>If models.dev comes to agree with a correction, the build reports it as redundant so it can be removed.</li>
      <li>If the entry a correction targets disappears upstream, the correction is not applied and no entry is recreated from it. It is listed under <a href="/evidence/#issues">open issues</a> and in <code>/api/v1/meta</code>.</li>
      <li>Every correction carries a reason and at least one source with a URL and a date. A build without them fails.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The same inputs always produce the same output, and the dataset version is a hash of that output.</p>

    <h2 id="sync">Daily import</h2>
    <p>
      Once a day a scheduled job fetches models.dev once, with a conditional request. The import is validated before it replaces anything: it must be valid JSON in the expected structure, ids
      must match their keys, and it must not be empty or have lost a large share of its entries since the previous import. If validation fails or models.dev is unavailable, the previous
      snapshot stays in place and keeps being served; the footer and <code>/api/v1/meta</code> report the failed import. Visitors and API requests never trigger a fetch.
    </p>
    <p>The current import, source commit and counts are published at <a href="/api/v1/meta"><code>/api/v1/meta</code></a>.</p>

    <h2 id="caching">Documented caching</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automatic</strong> (<code>implicit</code> in the data): the provider caches the start of a prompt by itself. The request does nothing for it.</li>
      <li><strong>Manual</strong> (<code>explicit</code>): nothing is cached unless the request marks what to cache. The mark also picks the lifetime, where there is a choice.</li>
      <li>
        On an entry's page the <em>Cache modes</em> table has one row for each way of caching, supported or not, with its minimum prompt size, breakpoints and targets. The lifetimes the
        provider documents are in the <em>Lifetimes</em> table (see <a href="#lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</a>), each naming its way of caching and the text as documented
        (<code>5m</code>, say). The request parameter and accepted values are recorded separately under Retention.
      </li>
      <li>An entry can have both: it caches by itself, and a request can also mark parts. Then each has its own line.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The pages render <code>providers_dev.cache.documented</code>. An omitted mode remains unknown; only an explicit negative makes it unsupported.</p>

    <h2 id="evaluations">Cache evaluations</h2>
    <p>
      An evaluation sends a fresh prompt once so that it is cached, then sends it again at once and at each of several set ages, a few times per age. For each age the result records how
      many of those re-sends were made, how many were served from the cache, and that share in percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every evaluation made since 14 November 2026 follows the same ages: the prompt is sent again at once, then checked at 2.5, 5 and 7.5 minutes, and on from there (10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 40
      and 50 minutes, 1 hour, 1 h 15, 1 h 30, 2 hours, and 4, 8, 12 and 24 hours where the cache lasts). "At once" is the control: it shows that the prompt was cached at all and that the
      route reports cache hits, so that a later miss means the cache is gone and not that nothing was cached. Older evaluations keep the ages they were made with, and some start at 5 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Results are shown per client. On an entry's page each measured lifetime in the <em>Lifetimes</em> table is one client and one request setting, with its results by age, and
      opens onto each of its evaluations with their own results. The columns are the control and the ages above. A cell is the share of re-sends at exactly that age that were served
      from the cache, in percent rounded to a whole number, with the hits and checks under it; the hover text has the exact percentage. Nothing is rounded into a neighbouring age:
      7.5 minutes and 10 minutes are two cells. A cell with no check shows a dot; it is not a miss. A check made at an age that has no column (an older evaluation at 3 hours, say) is in
      the data at its exact age and is listed in words on the entry's page. The evidence page lists every evaluation, each with its percentages by age, rounded the same way.
    </p>
    <p>There are two retention profiles, because a request can ask for the cache in two ways, and the two answer different questions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short:</strong> the request asks for no lifetime, or for the shortest one the provider documents. This is the cache you get without asking.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Long:</strong> the request asks for a longer lifetime, for example <code>cache_control.ttl=1h</code> or <code>prompt_cache_retention=24h</code>. Each measured lifetime
        shows the field and value its requests sent.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both profiles are there on every entry. A profile with no measurement says <em>Not evaluated</em>; that is not 0 %, and it says nothing about whether the provider accepts a
      lifetime. What a request can ask for is a separate record, <em>Retention</em>, on every entry's page: whether a lifetime is accepted, the request field, the accepted values and which
      value gives which line. It is filled from the provider's own documentation, quoted with the record: the field as a request sends it (<code>cache_control.ttl</code>, say), its
      values, and the default. Where the short lifetime is what a request gets by leaving the field out, the record says so instead of naming a value. Sending a value, receiving a cache
      hit, or listing several documented lifetimes is not enough to call a field supported, and nothing is copied from one provider to another: a reseller of the same model stays{" "}
      <em>Unknown</em> until its own page says it. Requested settings stay visible in evaluations even when support is unknown. The results show observed behavior, not a guaranteed lifetime.
    </p>
    <p>
      The evaluations of one client with one request setting are added up by exact age; the results of different clients are never added together. The entry's page lists every
      evaluation, with its client, request settings, counts and raw result. An evaluation that checked no age and whose control hit shows that the prompt was cached and nothing about
      how long: it is listed under <em>Other evaluations</em> and is in no lifetime.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every number on these pages is in the API as published, counts, percentages and lifetimes alike; a page formats it and works out nothing of its own.
      See <a href="/docs/api/#cache">cache evaluations in the API</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each evaluation names the access method it was taken on and the client that sent the requests: straight to the provider, through{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/router-for-me/CLIProxyAPI" rel="noopener">CLIProxyAPI</a> (an open-source proxy, and for a subscription login often the only way in besides the
      vendor's own tool), or through the vendor's own command-line tool. A result holds for that combination on that date. The evaluation source repository is listed in <a href="/api/v1/meta"><code>/api/v1/meta</code></a>, and every evaluation links to its raw result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry has a safe cache lifetime for each retention profile, short and long: how long a cached prompt can be relied on. It is worked out once, when the data is built, from
      the entry's documented lifetimes and its evaluations, and published in the API (<code>providers_dev.cache.lifetime</code>, and <code>cache_lifetime</code> of a summary). The
      pages print it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Documented.</strong> Where the provider documents a lifetime for the profile, that is the safe lifetime, unless a measurement clearly contradicts it.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contradicted.</strong> A measurement contradicts the documentation when one evaluation has a result at an age up to the documented lifetime, the immediate re-send
        included, in which fewer than half of the checks hit. Exactly half does not contradict. A miss after the documented lifetime is what the documentation says and is not looked at.
        Then the measured lifetime is the safe one, and the documented lifetime stays in the list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measured.</strong> Where nothing is documented, the safe lifetime is the measured one: for each evaluation, the oldest age up to which every check hit, from the
        immediate re-send on (one miss ends it, and an older age that hits again does not raise it); then the lowest of these across evaluations and clients.
      </li>
      <li>The safe lifetime is never above the documented lifetime: a cache that still hits after it changes nothing.</li>
      <li>
        A provider that documents that it does not cache has a safe lifetime of 0, documented, shown as <em>None</em>. A measured 0, shown as <em>0 s</em>, means no tested age at which
        every check hit. Where nothing is documented or measured the lifetime is not known and is shown as <em>Unknown</em>, never as 0.
      </li>
      <li>A measured lifetime is per client and request setting. It does not expire, and shows the date of its newest evaluation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On an entry's page the <em>Lifetimes</em> table gives, for each retention profile, the safe lifetime with its basis (documented or measured), source and date; then the documented
      lifetime, the measured one with its number of clients, and how many checks hit inside the documented lifetime. Where the safe lifetime is measured and a documented lifetime
      exists, the profile says <em>Documentation contradicted</em>. Where it is documented and a measurement exists, it says <em>Documentation not contradicted</em>: no evaluation
      contradicts it, which is not the same as confirmed. Under the profile, every documented and every measured lifetime, one per line, each with its source and date. The catalog, a
      provider's page and a model's page show the safe lifetime of both profiles in one <em>Cache lifetime</em> column, with the other of the two lifetimes beside it where there is one.
      Lifetimes are shown in minutes and hours (<em>5 min</em>, <em>24 h</em>); the exact seconds are in the hover text and in the API.
    </p>
    <p>
      The safe lifetime has limits. A measurement is a sample: a few requests from one account, on some days. An age between two tested ages is not known: a cache that hit at 30
      minutes and missed at 1 h 5 min may have ended at any point between. And a provider can change its cache behaviour without notice. The safe lifetime is a published number made by
      this rule, not a guarantee.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stale">Dates and staleness</h2>
    <p>
      Every correction and evaluation has a date. Providers change cache behaviour without notice, so evidence older than {STALE_AFTER_DAYS} days is marked <span class="stale">stale</span>. It
      stays published, with its date.
    </p>
    <p>Licences and attribution are on the <a href="/docs/licensing/">licensing</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</Doc>
